WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.033
Every claim now meets a
gate that reads your proof.

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:05.400
The next gate asks whether you
may write to this person at all.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.967
One withdrawn consent, and every
engine and every agent stops.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.900
No spreadsheet of who said what.

00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.533
By the end you'll record consent per
channel, write suppression rules,

00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:18.433
and erase a person with proof.
Retention and buying roles close it out.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.867
Sophia gets an erasure request.

00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.267
Weeks later somebody is still
hunting for copies of that person,

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:25.100
and nobody can prove the hunt ended.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.533
Set the rules once, and every
engine and agent follows them.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.733
First, consent per channel, because
a send needs a yes you can show.

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.667
In the console: CRM, Contacts,
open a contact, Consent tab.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:41.400
You'll see a line each for email,
phone, S M S, WhatsApp and LinkedIn.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.000
Each has a status, a legal basis such
as consent or legitimate interest,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.133
a source, proof and an expiry.

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:51.700
The history only grows, so
every change shows who made it.

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:52.833
Now how it blocks.

00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:56.667
Withdrawn, denied and expired
stop a send in every organization,

00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:59.433
and in G D P R mode a missing
legal basis stops it too.

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:03.667
The check reads every record for that
channel, and the strictest one wins,

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.400
whatever its source or purpose.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.967
A withdrawal for all purposes
beats a grant for one.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:12.633
It runs when a send is proposed and
again when it's sent, for sequences,

00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:14.533
LinkedIn steps, plays and agents alike.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.467
Or ask your agent: "Can we email
Priya Shah, and on what basis?

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:19.533
What about LinkedIn?

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:23.500
" You get what's allowed right now,
per channel, and why. Here's the trap.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.867
An agent can record a withdrawal or a
refusal. It can never record a grant.

00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:30.467
Only a person says yes
on someone's behalf.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.900
Second, G D P R mode, the switch that
turns a missing basis into a stop.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.800
In the console: Settings,
Brand and compliance.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.967
Owners and admins flip it, and
only a person can. While it's on,

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.867
no engine or agent contacts
or enriches someone

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:46.933
until a lawful basis is
recorded for that channel.

00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:48.933
New organizations start with it on.

00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:51.733
Existing ones stay off
until an owner decides.

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:55.567
Third, suppression rules, the people
your engines must never contact.

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:58.667
In the console: Setup,
Rules and who approves what.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.467
They cover competitors,
existing customers,

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:04.333
and a do not contact list
of emails and whole domains,

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.200
and every send is checked against them.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.833
Setup drafts them from your documents,
and a second person approves.

00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:13.100
Owners and admins see the list,
everyone else sees counts,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.600
and unsubscribes and
bounces add themselves.

00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:19.300
Or ask your agent: "Add acme-rival.
com to our do not contact rules".

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.100
It proposes the change and
returns an approval number.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.033
Nothing moves until another
owner or admin approves,

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:27.800
and the approval is bound
to the exact payload,

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.767
so what they approve is what lands.

00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:32.833
Fourth, erase, the answer to
a right to erasure request.

00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:35.833
It deletes the person's record,
identity keys, search entries,

00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:39.400
and related rows such as
messages and timeline,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.500
then redacts the observations behind them.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.800
Then it plants a guard, so nothing
quietly ingests them again.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.300
The guard is a hash, computed before
the identity keys are deleted,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.900
so it recognizes the person
without remembering them.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.100
The erase, the log and the guard
commit together or not at all.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.600
There is no undo. The erasure log
keeps counts, hashes, who and when.

00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:01.400
Run it through your agent,
and start with a dry run:

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.767
"Dry run an erasure for Priya Shah
and show me what would be deleted".

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.567
It lists the impact and changes nothing.

00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:09.367
Pro move: read that
list before you decide.

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:12.167
The erase itself is
deliberate and permanent.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:15.100
Afterwards, ask for the
erasure log. That's your proof.

00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:19.300
Fifth, retention, how long raw observations live, set by class: ephemeral,

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.433
standard or sensitive.

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:25.267
A sweep redacts older ones and keeps
the hash and the source locator.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.167
Zero keeps them forever,

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:31.100
and archived records have their own
retention in Settings, C R M settings.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.167
Or ask your agent: "What retention
policy do we have for each class? "

00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:39.233
Last, buying roles, so every engine
means the same thing by champion.

00:03:39.233 --> 00:03:43.200
In the console: Settings, Brand
and compliance, Buying roles.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.100
The defaults are champion, economic
buyer, decision maker, influencer, user,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.433
blocker and unknown, and
you can add your own.

00:03:50.433 --> 00:03:53.800
Common spellings, such as
budget holder, are normalized,

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.600
so old data keeps its
meaning without a rewrite.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.633
Or ask your agent: "Which
buying roles do we use,

00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:02.700
and who is the champion on
the Northwind renewal? "

00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:04.300
Someone on your team will say:

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.333
an agent with access to my contacts
could grant itself consent. It can't.

00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:12.533
Agents can only tighten consent, by
recording a withdrawal or a refusal.

00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:13.833
A grant needs a person.

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.767
And the gate reads the stored
record when the send executes,

00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:18.900
so a claim in a chat changes nothing.

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:22.333
Say it back: Consent is recorded
per channel and checked twice.

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:25.433
Suppression rules and G D
P R mode block the rest.

00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:28.667
Erase comes with a dry run and proof.
An agent can take consent away.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.367
Only a person can give it.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.867
Compliance now runs without
anyone remembering it.

00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:35.200
Open one contact and read its Consent tab.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.267
Next is Score: the engine that ranks
every account and shows its reasons.
